LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Developed by design, not found", "13", "<p>Module 5 ended every appraisal with a development plan; this module builds the machinery that delivers them — and its thesis confronts the trade's favourite hiring myth head-on: <b>people are developed by design, not found.</b></p><p><b>The hire-ready myth.</b> The belief the consultant hears in every engagement: <i>we just need to find good people</i> — as if the market stocks ready-made storekeepers who count before signing, till operators who close clean, and accountants who run day-four closes, and the firm's only job is shopping well. The market rarely sells ready-made — it sells RAW MATERIAL of varying quality (module 2's pipeline selects the best of it: aptitude, integrity, the instincts the practical test surfaces), and everything after selection is MANUFACTURE: the disciplines this academy spent four tracks defining are learned behaviours, taught deliberately or absorbed accidentally, and the firm that relies on absorption gets exactly the inconsistency it observes — the branch whose standards live in whoever trained whom, informally, years ago. The reframe for owners: hiring buys potential; training converts it — and the conversion is the firm's job, because nobody else will do it to the firm's standard.</p><p><b>Training as infrastructure, not event.</b> The second reframe: training in the unstructured firm is an EVENT (the workshop someone attended once, the induction that happened to early staff and faded, the vendor session at go-live) — and events decay, because people join after them, forget past them, and drift beyond them. Training as INFRASTRUCTURE is the standing machinery: curricula that exist per role (Chapter 3), delivered on demand to every joiner and every promotion (the onboarding path of module 2; the development plans of module 5), assessed so completion means competence (Chapter 5's whole argument), refreshed as the firm's practices evolve, and READ like every other layer (Chapter 6's measurement). The economics repeat the academy's oldest trade: the curriculum built once teaches forever — the storm of every-hire-improvised-induction becomes the weather of a path assigned in an afternoon.</p><p><b>The meta note, made explicit.</b> This module has an unusual property the consultant should notice and use: the machinery it describes is the machinery DELIVERING it — a role-based curriculum, structured modules, proctored assessment, certification with serials — because the academy this certification belongs to IS the pattern at work: the consultant reading this chapter inside a training path, tracked by completion, gated by a proctored bank, is holding the proof that the pattern scales from a software vendor's consultant corps to a three-branch pharmacy's till roster. The pitch to clients writes itself: <i>the system that trained me can train your people — same machinery, your content</i> — and Chapter 3 will show exactly how the pattern transfers.</p><p><b>The scope line.</b> What this module covers: needs, curricula, delivery, certification, measurement, and paths — the people-development machinery. What stays at its boundaries: the statutory training obligations of regulated roles (the superintendent pharmacist's continuing-education requirements — tracked here, defined by the regulator and the professional), and the formal-education sponsorships with their bonds (module 4's study-leave machinery holding that edge). The module's map follows capability's life: found (needs), built (curricula and delivery), proven (certification), verified (measurement), and grown (paths) — the manufacture, end to end.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The training needs picture", "13", "<p>Design starts from needs, and the needs are not guessed — they are READ, from sources the firm already runs. The detection chapter.</p><p><b>The skill map: the reference grid.</b> The foundation artifact: per ROLE (module 1's designation list earning another dividend), the required competences listed — the till role's (the sale, the return, the cash-up, the disputed-price handling, the till policies), the storekeeper's (receiving discipline, counting, the custody documents, the location system), the accountant's (the close's lines, the reconciliations, the correction doctrine) — at the levels the role needs (aware / competent / able-to-teach: three levels serve the trade; five serve consultancies). The map is the layer everything else references: the GAP analysis (each person against their role's row — the visual that turns 'training needs' from a mood into a list), the onboarding path's syllabus (the role's row IS the curriculum outline — Chapter 3), and the succession read (Chapter 7's single-point capabilities are the map's columns with one name in them). Built once with the managers (a half-day per role family), maintained as roles evolve — the map is a master, governed like one.</p><p><b>The intake channels.</b> Where needs arrive from, each channel already flowing: <b>appraisal development plans</b> (module 5's forward half — the plans aggregated per season become the training demand book: twelve people needing the costing module is a scheduled cohort, not twelve promises), <b>the operational reads as needs detectors</b> (the academy's quiet gift to this module: the error CLUSTERS are needs wearing incident clothes — the receiving discipline slipping at one branch is a refresher flag before it is a disciplinary question; the correction-log entries clustering on one journal type is a finance-training finding; module 5's improvement cases arriving with their gaps pre-named), <b>onboarding</b> (every hire and promotion triggering the role's path — module 2's checklist line, feeding this machinery), <b>compliance and regulation</b> (the regulated trade's mandated competences — the pharmacy's requirements, the fire-and-safety layer, the certifications with expiry dates Chapter 5 tracks), and <b>the firm's own changes</b> (the new product line, the new module going live, the policy that changed — every change this academy's other tracks taught the firm to make as an event carries a training line in its rollout, which is why the corridor-toggle conviction kept insisting changes be announced: the announcement IS micro-training).</p><p><b>The needs register.</b> The channels converge on one governed list: the training needs register — each need with its source (the appraisal, the read, the regulation), its population (who needs it), its priority (the compliance need outranks the aspiration; the bleeding read outranks both), and its disposition (the path assigned, the event scheduled, the cohort formed — or the honest deferral with a date). Read quarterly (module 8's calendar), the register converts development from a sentiment into a pipeline — and its aggregate tells its own story: the register dominated by remedial needs is a firm whose entries leak (module 2's early-attrition logic applied to competence); the one dominated by growth needs is a firm whose foundation holds. The consultant reads the register's SHAPE before its lines — the diagnosis-before-prescription habit, at the capability layer.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The curriculum per role: training paths", "14", "<p>The needs become teachable through CURRICULA — the role-based paths that turn the skill map's rows into structured, assessable journeys. The design chapter, and the pattern this academy itself proves.</p><p><b>The path's anatomy.</b> Per role (or role family), the training path: MODULES in sequence (each covering one competence area — the till path's product module, sales module, till-operations module, policies module; the storekeeper's receiving, counting, custody, and systems modules), each module holding LESSONS (the readable units — text, images, the short video where it earns its place: the mock delivery filmed once teaches forever) and its ASSESSMENT (Chapter 5's machinery — the quiz that gates completion), the path ordered by dependency (the product knowledge before the sale; the document flow before the exception handling), and sized honestly (the till path a new hire completes inside probation; the consultant path a quarter's journey — the path that cannot be completed alongside the job is a path that won't be).</p><p><b>Content from the firm's own truth.</b> The design law that separates working curricula from shelfware: the content teaches THE FIRM'S actual practice — its documents, its policies, its screens, its scenarios (the disputed-price lesson uses the firm's price-dispute policy; the receiving lesson walks the firm's actual receiving flow with its actual forms) — because generic content teaches generic behaviour, and the gap between the course and the counter is where training dies. The sourcing economics: the firm's practices are ALREADY WRITTEN (four tracks of policy pages, checklists, and process documents — the academy's documentation-as-byproduct thesis paying its development dividend: the curriculum is the policy page, taught), and the maintenance discipline follows (the policy that changes updates its lesson — the curriculum versioned with the practices it teaches, or it quietly becomes a museum of the old way).</p><p><b>Assessment as the gate.</b> The path's spine: each module ASSESSED (the question bank per module, the pass mark, the proctored mode where stakes warrant — timed, served from a bank large enough that retakes meet fresh questions), completion meaning DEMONSTRATED (Chapter 5 will hammer this: attendance is not learning; the certificate that only proves presence gates nothing safely), and retakes governed (allowed — the goal is competence, not filtration — with the retake pattern read: the module everyone fails twice is a content problem or a bank problem, and the read fixes the course before it blames the class).</p><p><b>Assignment and progress.</b> The path's lifecycle in the machinery: ASSIGNED at the trigger (onboarding's checklist line; the appraisal's development plan; the promotion's new-role requirements — module 7's transfer machinery will add its own trigger), tracked visibly (the person sees their path and progress in self-service — the same transparency dividend as every layer; the manager sees the team's — the path stalled at module two for a month is a check-in topic, not a mystery), and COMPLETED into the record (the completions and scores landing in module 5's evidence folder and Chapter 5's certification register — training results as performance evidence, the loop this track keeps closing). The meta note made operational: this paragraph describes the exact machinery serving this exact lesson — assignment, progress, proctored gates, records — and the consultant configuring it for a client is installing the pattern they are currently inside.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Programs & events: the delivery machinery", "13", "<p>Paths carry the standing curriculum; PROGRAMS and EVENTS carry the live layer — the sessions, drills, and cohorts where people learn together. The delivery chapter.</p><p><b>The internal session: the trade's workhorse.</b> The firm teaching itself, structured: the branch practical (the mock delivery run at the actual gate — Inventory's receiving discipline drilled with a staged carton and a deliberately short document; the till drill on the training instance — the disputed price, the shortpay, the cash-up rehearsed where mistakes are free), the classroom hour (the policy walkthrough, the new-module introduction), and the toolbox session (the fifteen-minute shift-start refresher — the lightest unit, and for the trade often the highest-yield: one scenario, one reminder, done before the doors open). The design disciplines: sessions have OBJECTIVES (what the attendee can DO after that they couldn't before — the session without one is a meeting wearing a flipchart), MATERIALS kept (the session pack filed — taught once, reusable), and the practical bias the trade demands (the ratio tilted toward doing: the receiving lesson that is 80% watching is 20% learning).</p><p><b>The trainer layer: teaching as development.</b> The delivery force is the firm's own seniors — and the design insight with the double dividend: the senior storekeeper teaching receiving is simultaneously delivering training AND being developed (the able-to-teach level of Chapter 2's map is a competence in its own right — the teacher must articulate what they do by instinct, which deepens the instinct; and the teaching role is the pre-supervisory test module 7's ladder will read). The governance: trainers are NAMED per module (the map's able-to-teach column populated deliberately), prepared (the trainer's session pack, the first delivery observed), and recognised (the teaching noted in module 5's recognition layer — the firm that treats teaching as extra work unpaid teaches its seniors to hoard knowledge, which is the single-point disease being actively cultivated).</p><p><b>The training event as a record.</b> Every delivery documented — the event record: what was taught (the module, the version), by whom, to whom (the attendance captured — module 3's discipline reaching the classroom), with what assessment outcome (the post-session check where the content warrants one), and filed. The record's consumers make the case for the discipline: the compliance audit (the regulated trade asked WHO was trained on the safety procedure, WHEN — the register answers or the firm improvises), the incident review (the error's actor trained or not trained is the first fork in every root-cause tree — trained-and-erred is coaching; never-trained-and-erred is the FIRM'S error wearing the person's name), and the measurement layer (Chapter 6 needs the before/after line, and the event record is the 'after' begins here marker).</p><p><b>External programs.</b> The bought layer, governed: the external course selected against the needs register (not the brochure's charisma), the attendee BRIEFED with the reuse expectation (the returning attendee teaches the toolbox session — the bought knowledge multiplied instead of filed), the certificate landing in Chapter 5's register with its expiry where it carries one, and the spend read annually against Chapter 6's evidence (the external budget that produces no behaviour change buys travel, not training). The sizing honesty as always: the eleven-person firm's delivery machinery is a senior, a checklist, and a staged carton — the SHAPE, at teaspoon scale, and fully sufficient.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Certification & competence: records that gate authority", "14", "<p>The layer that converts training from activity into ASSURANCE: certification — the proven-competence record — and its highest use: gating authority. The chapter where this track meets every other track's permission designs.</p><p><b>Internal certification: completion means demonstrated.</b> The proctored assessment as the gate (the timed quiz served from the bank, the pass mark set to mean something, the certificate issued with its SERIAL — the register's law, at the competence layer: numbered, dated, revocable, never reissued casually), and the conviction the chapter hammers: <b>attendance is not learning</b> — the sign-in sheet proves presence; the assessment proves competence; and everything this chapter gates (the authorities below) must gate on the assessment, because gating on attendance is gating on nothing wearing a lanyard. The certification's scope is stated on its face (certified for WHAT — the module, the version, the role level), and re-certification exists where practices drift or stakes demand currency (the annual re-take for the safety layer; the version-triggered refresh when the policy that was taught changed).</p><p><b>External certifications: tracked with expiries.</b> The bought and regulated layer in the same register: the professional licences (the pharmacist's — the regulated trade's trading licence wearing a person's name), the statutory certificates (fire safety, first aid, the forklift), and the vendor certifications — each recorded against the person with its EXPIRY, feeding module 1's expiry worklist (the machinery already built: surfaced 60 and 30 days out, owned, worked), because the expired licence discovered by the inspector is the compliance failure that was a calendar entry all along. The regulated-role read: the roles that legally REQUIRE current certification listed, the register's coverage checked monthly (the branch whose superintendent's licence lapses cannot lawfully trade its regulated lines — the certification register is trading infrastructure, exactly as the branch door's device was).</p><p><b>Competence gating authority: the chapter's summit.</b> The design pattern that makes this module load-bearing for the whole academy: the AUTHORITIES four tracks assigned — the closing-authorised roster (module 3's till count and cash-up), the adjust permission's two names (Inventory 8), the journal pen (Accounts 2), the receiving sign-off, the price-override right — each maps to a COMPETENCE, and the mature firm wires the mapping: <b>authority follows certification</b> — the closing roster admits the till-certified; the adjustment names hold the counting certification; the new branch's authority matrix (Inventory 6's playbook) is staffed from the certified register, not from availability. The dividend runs both directions: the authority granted on proven competence is defensible (the incident review that finds a certified actor asks different questions than one that finds an improvised grant), and the certification WANTED because it unlocks (the development path acquires teeth — the ambitious till assistant knows exactly what stands between them and the closing roster, and it is a curriculum, not a relationship: the anti-favouritism machinery of this whole track, expressed as a syllabus).</p><p><b>The certified register.</b> The layer's standing artifact: who holds what, current, per branch and role — the coverage view (every authority's certified population per branch: the single-certified capability is Chapter 7's succession flag raised automatically), the expiry horizon (the quarter's renewals visible), and the audit face (the register that answers the compliance question, the incident question, and the staffing question from one page). Governed as a master, read monthly, and joined everywhere — the fifth master's competence shadow, and the page module 8's analytics will summarise.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Measuring training: did it work?", "13", "<p>The layer's honesty chapter: training consumes hours and money, and the firm deserves to know whether it changed anything. Measurement at working altitude — the levels, the reads, and the redesign discipline.</p><p><b>The levels, at trade altitude.</b> The classic evaluation ladder, sized for a trading firm: <b>reaction</b> (did attendees rate it useful — the lightest signal, collected in a minute, predictive of little but worth having: the session everyone rated confusing needs its content read before its results), <b>learning</b> (the assessment scores — Chapter 5's gates producing this level automatically: pass rates, score distributions, the retake patterns), <b>behaviour</b> (the level that matters most and costs least in THIS firm — because the operational reads ARE behaviour measurement: the receiving discipline's error rate, the session variance rate, the count accuracy, the correction-log clusters — all captured continuously by machinery four tracks installed, so the question <i>did the training change behaviour?</i> is answered by reads the firm already runs), and <b>results</b> (the business numbers the behaviour serves — the shrinkage band, the close's day count, the collection rate: moved by many causes, read with that honesty, but read).</p><p><b>The before/after discipline.</b> The measurement pattern the consultant installs: the training intervention BOOKENDED — the baseline read taken before (the branch's receiving error rate for the quarter preceding), the intervention delivered and dated (Chapter 4's event record supplying the marker), the after read taken at the stated horizon (the following quarter's rate), and the movement attributed with appropriate modesty (the confound named where it exists — the same quarter's staff change, the season's volume shift; the honest sentence is <i>the rate moved from X to Y across the intervention; here is what else moved</i> — the epistemics of Accounts 6's variance decomposition, applied to people). The pattern's cheapness is its argument: both reads already exist; the discipline is only the bookending — dated, filed, compared.</p><p><b>Training ROI in the trade's own numbers.</b> The owner conversation, equipped: the counting refresher priced (the trainer's afternoon, the attendees' hours) against the variance trend it moved (the shrinkage naira, the recount hours saved); the till drill against the session-variance decline; the pipeline's practical-test training against the early-attrition rate — the returns denominated in the same naira the reads already count, which is what makes the case land where 'training is important' never does. The honest boundary stated with it: not everything measurable matters and not everything that matters is measurable (the service culture, the confidence of the confirmed) — the numbers carry the argument they can carry, and the qualitative half gets its qualitative account, exactly as module 5's goal sheets ruled.</p><p><b>When training didn't work: redesign, not repeat.</b> The failure discipline: the intervention whose after-read didn't move gets DIAGNOSED, not rescheduled — the fork tree: wrong diagnosis (the error cluster wasn't a knowledge gap — the equipment, the rota, the incentive: the training that teaches what people already know treats a system problem with a syllabus), wrong content (the generic course that never touched the firm's actual flow — Chapter 3's design law violated), wrong delivery (the watching-heavy session for a doing-heavy skill), or wrong gate (the pass mark that certified the incompetent — Chapter 5's bank and mark reviewed). The conviction closing the chapter: repeating failed training is the corridor toggle of the capability layer — the same intervention, expecting different behaviour, consuming goodwill each round — and the read-diagnose-redesign loop is what separates a training FUNCTION from a training calendar.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The development path & succession", "13", "<p>The module's forward horizon: capability built not just for today's role but for the next one — the ladder, the deputy, and the succession discipline that applies the two-names law to capabilities. The growth chapter.</p><p><b>The ladder per role family.</b> The visible progression: the till family (assistant → senior till → counter supervisor), the warehouse family (storekeeper → senior storekeeper → ops lead), the finance family (Accounts 8's bookkeeper → accountant → controller, now with its training spine), the branch family (the supervisor → branch manager path the multi-branch firm must manufacture — Inventory 6's day-one-capable branch manager does not exist in the market at the firm's standard; the firm builds them or borrows them badly). Each rung DEFINED (the skill map's rows at the next level — what the senior storekeeper must hold that the storekeeper needn't; the certifications the rung requires — Chapter 5's gating giving the ladder its teeth), and the definition PUBLISHED: the ladder everyone can see converts ambition into curriculum (module 5's promotion-from-folders culture, given its syllabus — the anti-proximity machinery completed).</p><p><b>Development plans feeding ladders.</b> The connection wired: module 5's development plans reference the ladder (the plan that says 'grow toward senior storekeeper' decomposes into the rung's named gaps — the modules to pass, the teaching to deliver, the exposure to gain), the path assigned accordingly (Chapter 3's machinery), and progress read at the check-ins (module 5's cadence carrying the development conversation, not just the performance one). The pace honesty: ladders are climbed in years, not quarters — the plan promising a rung annually to everyone is writing cheques the org structure cannot cash, and the honest ladder conversation includes the arithmetic (rungs open when the structure grows or seniors move — module 1's vanity-structure conviction guarding this exact door: the title manufactured to reward is the grade structure being spent like petty cash).</p><p><b>Succession: the two-names law for capabilities.</b> The chapter's summit, and the roster's single-point discipline (module 3) applied to the capability layer: every CRITICAL capability — the certified register's columns that keep branches trading and books closing (the closing authority, the receiving sign-off, the close's owner, the superintendent's license-bearing role) — carries the question <i>who is the second name?</i> — and the succession read (quarterly, off the certified register: the capabilities with one current holder listed automatically) drives deliberate deputy development: the second person trained BEFORE the vacancy (the deputy who learned during the maternity cover — module 4's cover plan doubling as succession machinery; the senior developed against the ladder before the ops-lead need opens), because the alternative is the trade's familiar scramble — the resignation that orphans an authority, the promotion delayed by its own vacuum, the branch that cannot trade a Thursday. The framing for owners, in the academy's oldest voice: succession is not disloyalty planning; it is the handover test (every track's module 8) applied to people — the firm whose capabilities survive their holders has solved at the human layer what the documentation solved at the process layer.</p><p><b>Mentoring, formalised lightly.</b> The pattern module 1's spine already carried (the Ibadan manager mentoring Akure): named pairs (the mentor chosen for the able-to-teach level and the temperament), a cadence (monthly, protected), a scope (the rung being climbed — the mentor walks their own old path), and the recognition loop (mentoring recorded as the senior contribution it is — module 5's log; Chapter 4's trainer dividend, extended). The chapter's closing line joins the thesis to the firm's future: the ladder is the curriculum's shadow, the deputy is the succession's answer, and both exist because the firm decided to manufacture its next generation instead of hoping to find it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's development year", "16", "<p>The capability machinery, installed and run. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The map and the register (Ch. 2).</b> The skill map built in three half-days with the managers: five role families, competences at three levels, the able-to-teach column populated honestly (thin — two names covering most of it: the single-point finding arriving before the succession chapter needed it). The needs register convenes its channels: the appraisal season's development plans (module 5's intake — eleven plans aggregating into four cohort needs), one operational-read flag (the correction log clustering on expense journals — a finance refresher scheduled), and the compliance layer confirmed (the superintendent's licence current, two fire-safety certificates expiring in Q3 — into the worklist).</p><p><b>The curricula deployed (Ch. 3).</b> The till and storekeeper paths built from the firm's own pages — the policy documents, receiving checklists, and till procedures of four tracks becoming lessons in a fortnight (the documentation-as-byproduct dividend, cashed), each module gated by a proctored bank, sized to complete inside probation. Assigned to the two Akure hires mid-probation (their confirmation objectives already referencing the modules — module 2's machinery meshing), and to two existing staff whose gap analysis showed holes the informal era left. The retake read catches one content fault: the counting module's question everyone misses twice — a badly-anchored question, fixed in the bank, not blamed on the class.</p><p><b>The delivery layer (Ch. 4).</b> The trainer roles named: the Ibadan storekeeper (the clean-cycle-count quarter of module 5's recognition) prepared and observed teaching the receiving practical — the staged carton, the deliberately short document — and the toolbox pattern lands at the branches: fifteen minutes, one scenario, before the Saturday doors. The event records file. The teaching dividend shows inside a quarter: the trainer's own receiving notes sharpen (<i>articulating the instinct deepened it</i> — the debrief line), and his log gains the senior-contribution entries the ladder will read.</p><p><b>Certification gates authority (Ch. 5).</b> The certified register stands up, and the wiring completes: the closing-authorised roster (HR 3's Akure fix) now admits by till certification — the ambitious Akure till assistant (module 2's quiet candidate) sees the syllabus between her and the closing roster, completes it in six weeks, certifies, and joins the roster: <i>a curriculum, not a relationship</i> — the sentence the branch remembers. The adjustment names re-verified against counting certifications; the register's coverage view flags the single-certified capabilities for Chapter 7's read.</p><p><b>Measurement and succession (Ch. 6-7).</b> The receiving refresher bookended: Akure's receiving error rate read for the prior quarter, the intervention dated, the after-read two quarters on — the rate halves, the confound named honestly (the same period's second hire), the ROI sentence filed in the trade's naira. And the succession read runs off the register: three single-name capabilities listed automatically — the close's owner, the receiving sign-off at Lagos, the ops-lead capability that doesn't exist yet — and the year's deliberate answer: the Ibadan storekeeper's development plan formalises toward ops lead (the ladder's rung defined, the mentor named — the MD himself, monthly, protected), the Lagos deputy trained before any vacancy asks, and the maternity cover of module 4 retro-recognised as the succession machinery it accidentally was. The MD's year-note: <i>we used to hope the market would sell us our next ops lead; we are building him instead — and he taught the class that proved he could.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the sixth law", "13", "<p>The capability layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — training as event.</b> Symptom: the workshop attended once; the induction that faded after the founding staff; go-live training decaying with every hire since. Disease: events where infrastructure belongs (Ch. 1) — training that people join after, forget past, drift beyond. Fix: the standing curricula per role, assigned at every entry and promotion, versioned with the practices — the path, not the workshop.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the hire-ready myth.</b> Symptom: 'we just need good people'; every gap answered by recruitment; the standards living in whoever trained whom informally. Disease: shopping where manufacture belongs (Ch. 1). Fix: the reframe (hiring buys potential; training converts it), the pipeline selecting raw quality, the curriculum doing the conversion — and the branch-consistency read as the proof the owner watches.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — certificates without competence.</b> Symptom: sign-in sheets as training records; authorities granted on attendance; the incident actor 'trained' by a register that only proves presence. Disease: attendance mistaken for learning (Ch. 5). Fix: assessment as the gate, proctored where stakes warrant, authority following certification — and the register's face stating what was PROVEN, not what was attended.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the untracked expiry.</b> Symptom: the licence discovered lapsed by the inspector; the branch trading regulated lines on an expired superintendent; the first-aider whose certificate died quietly. Disease: compliance as memory (Ch. 5) — the calendar entry that was never entered. Fix: every dated certificate in the register feeding the expiry worklist, the regulated-coverage read monthly, renewals owned — the certification register treated as the trading infrastructure it is.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the single-point capability.</b> Symptom: the one closer, the one signer, the one licence-holder; the resignation that orphans an authority; the Thursday the branch couldn't trade. Disease: the two-names law never applied to capabilities (Ch. 7). Fix: the succession read off the certified register (single-name columns flagged automatically), the deputy developed before the vacancy, cover plans read as succession machinery — capabilities that survive their holders.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — development promised, never delivered.</b> Symptom: appraisal plans that reference training which never happens; the same growth goals copied year to year; a workforce that learns the development plan is a pleasantry. Disease: the intake without the pipeline (Ch. 2) — module 5's forward half orphaned. Fix: the needs register with dispositions and dates, the plans aggregated into scheduled cohorts, the quarterly register read — and the credibility arithmetic taught to owners: one delivered plan buys more engagement than ten promised ones.</p><p><b>The sixth law.</b> The record, the pipeline, the capture, the entitlement, the conversation — and now the manufacture: <b>people are developed by design — needs read from the machinery the firm already runs, curricula built per role from the firm's own truth, delivery that biases toward doing, completion meaning demonstrated, authority following certification, results measured before and after, and every critical capability carrying a second name before the vacancy asks.</b> The ladder converts ambition into curriculum; the register gates authority on proof; and the firm that manufactures its people stops shopping for what the market doesn't sell. Module 7 takes the lifecycle to its events and its end: promotions, transfers, discipline, and the clean separation.</p>"),
]

